<article class="env-remark" id="0ERL">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0ERL">Remark <span data-tag="0ERL">82.26.3</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/0EDT" data-tag="0EDT">82.2.1</a> let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of good algebraic spaces over $B$. Then there is a canonical $\mathbf{Z}$-algebra map $A^*(Y) \to A^*(X)$. Namely, given $c \in A^ p(Y)$ and $X' \to X$, then we can let $f^*c$ be defined by the map $c \cap - : \mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits _ k(X') \to \mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits _{k - p}(X')$ which is given by thinking of $X'$ as an algebraic space over $Y$. </p>
